# Loom Node — Android app

An Android phone or tablet joins Loom by installing **one APK**, not by setting
up Termux. The app embeds the `loom-node` daemon and keeps it alive with a
foreground service, so the device becomes a Loom node that agents can run
commands on — the same device fabric as a Mac/Windows/Linux node.

Written from scratch (not a fork of an existing agent): four small Java files,
a manifest, and a gradle-free build script.

## How it works

- **Embedded daemon.** `loom-node` is cross-compiled `CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=android
  GOARCH=arm64` (pure Go, no NDK) and packed into the APK as
  `lib/arm64-v8a/libloomnode.so`. An app may exec a binary only out of its
  extracted native-library dir (the one spot not under the W^X exec ban), so the
  app launches `libloomnode.so daemon` from `applicationInfo.nativeLibraryDir`
  with `HOME` pointed at its private files dir (Go's `os.UserHomeDir()` honours
  `$HOME`, so the daemon's config/state land in the sandbox).
- **Keep-alive.** `NodeService` is a foreground service (type `dataSync`) with a
  partial wake-lock that supervises the daemon in a restart loop — the
  legitimate background pattern. `BootReceiver` restarts it after a reboot or an
  app update. On first connect the app shows the standard *ignore battery
  optimisation* dialog (one tap), which a plain app may do and Termux cannot.
- **DNS.** A CGO-free `GOOS=android` build has no `/etc/resolv.conf` and no
  system-property resolver, so Go's pure resolver falls back to `[::1]:53` and
  every lookup fails. `node/dns_android.go` points `net.DefaultResolver` at the
  `getprop net.dnsN` servers, else public resolvers. Without this the daemon
  cannot resolve Loom's host even though `curl` can.

## Files

- `MainActivity` — enter the install code (or paste the full install
  URL / the whole curl line), fetch the token, start the service, then ask for
  the battery-optimisation exemption. Starts the service **before** the dialog:
  the dialog backgrounds the activity, and Android 14 forbids starting a
  foreground service from the background.
- `NodeService` — the foreground service + supervisor loop.
- `BootReceiver` — restart on boot / app-replace, only if configured.
- `Config` — the paths, shared with the daemon.

## Onboarding

Make an install command in Loom's **Devices**; the one-time code is short-lived.
Three ways to use it, all carrying the same code:

- **curl** (macOS/Linux/WSL): `curl -fsSL <loom>/node/install/<code> | sh`
- **PowerShell** (Windows): `irm <loom>/node/install/<code>/ps1 | iex`
- **Android**: install this app, open it, type the `<code>`. The app downloads
  from `<loom>/node/app/<code>` (served only while the code is live) and redeems
  `<loom>/node/install/<code>` for the token.

The app accepts a bare code when a join origin is baked in at build time (see
below); otherwise paste a full install URL.

## Build

`sh build.sh` → `build/loom-node.apk`. Gradle-free:
aapt2 → javac → d8 → zipalign → apksigner. Needs a JDK, the Android SDK
(platform-34, build-tools 34.0.0) at `$ANDROID_HOME`, and host-arch
`aapt2`/`zipalign`. On arm64 the SDK ships only x86_64 aapt2/zipalign, so install
the distro's (`apt install aapt zipalign`); d8/apksigner are JVM wrappers.

- `$LOOM_JOIN_ORIGIN` (optional) is baked into `R.string.join_origin` so the app
  accepts a bare code; leave it empty to require a full URL. `build/loom.jks` is
  a self-signed key generated on first build. `build/`, `lib/`, and the generated
  `res/values/config.xml` are git-ignored (artifacts + signing key + a possibly
  private host).

`rel/deploy.sh` runs this when a toolchain is present and serves the APK at
`GET /node/app/:code`; a host without the toolchain still deploys.

## Field notes (what actually bit us)

- **OEM battery managers are a wall by design.** An unprivileged app (and Termux
  even more so) cannot add itself to the autostart/battery whitelist — that is
  the anti-"rogue app" mechanism. The app does what it can (foreground service +
  wake-lock + the battery-opt dialog); the rest is one-time manual grants:
  - **ColorOS / OnePlus:** Battery → allow background / unrestricted; App
    management → allow auto-launch; **lock the app in Recents** (swiping it away
    kills even a foreground service); then don't swipe it away.
  - **HarmonyOS / Huawei:** Settings → Apps → **App launch management** → turn
    off "manage automatically" → enable **auto-launch, secondary launch, run in
    background**; lock in Recents. This proprietary manager resists even ADB.
- **Doze cuts background network on screen-off** unless the app holds the
  battery-optimisation exemption; a partial wake-lock keeps the CPU but not the
  network. Grant the exemption or the node drops ~a minute after lock.
- **Limits that are not bugs:** `FLAG_SECURE` screens (banking/pay) read back
  black to screenshots; a locked screen cannot be unlocked by a non-privileged
  app; there is no way to auto-answer a runtime-permission dialog.
- iOS cannot be a node: no persistent background execution and no global
  accessibility for third-party apps.
